const representations = {
  title:"Structured English, flowcharts and pseudocode",
  subtitle:"Preserve operations, conditions and execution order when converting between three algorithm representations.",
  guidingQuestion:"Do all representations select the same output for the same input, including boundary cases?",
  diagnostic:{prompt:"When changing a flowchart into pseudocode, may a condition Age >= 18 be shortened to Age = 18?",answer:"No. The operator is part of the algorithm's meaning, so it must be preserved."},
  units:[
    unit("REPRESENTATIONS-ENGLISH","Document a solution in structured English",ids(7,1),[
      "Structured English expresses an algorithm using controlled natural-language steps and clear grouping. State the operations in order, identify both outcomes of a decision and make the scope of any repetition explicit. It should leave the same computational choices determined as pseudocode does.",
      "A flowchart shows that control structure with symbols and directed lines, while pseudocode uses statements and indentation. Changing notation should not change the accepted inputs or results. Trace a value on each side of a boundary when comparing two representations.",
    ],[flow("Age classification in structured English",[["Read","Read a person's age as a non-negative whole number."],["Decide","If the age is at least 18, display Adult; otherwise display Minor."],["Finish","End after displaying one classification."]]),example("age","Translate a written age rule","Write pseudocode for the displayed structured English.","The comparison remains >= 18. Ages 17, 18 and 19 produce Minor, Adult and Adult.")],
    "Structured English must describe the rule; 'process the age' is not a defined decision.","Must an ELSE outcome be preserved during conversion?","Yes, when the source specifies an alternative action for a false condition."),
    unit("REPRESENTATIONS-SYMBOLS","Use symbols and arrows consistently",ids(7,1),[
      "Use terminators for start and end, parallelograms for input and output, rectangles for processing, and diamonds for decisions. Arrowheads indicate the next operation. Label the outgoing decision paths consistently, for example Yes and No, so the truth value selects an unambiguous route.",
      "A loop needs a return path to its condition or repeated body, according to when it tests. Paths that rejoin must not imply that both selection branches execute. Inspect the arrow direction at the final output as well as at the decision; an output cannot follow an end terminator.",
    ],[{type:"reviewed-visual",title:"Standard flowchart symbols",asset:diagramPath("symbols"),alt:"Terminator for start or end; parallelogram for input and output; rectangle for processing; diamond for a decision; directed arrows for execution order.",facts:["Rounded terminators mark the beginning and end.","Parallelograms represent input and output.","Rectangles represent calculations and assignments.","Diamonds select labelled branches; arrowheads indicate execution direction."],review:"reviewed",caption:"A flowchart is a diagram of execution, so the direction and meaning of each connection matter."}],
    "A decorative arrow or an unlabelled split does not define a reliable execution path.","Which symbol holds Total <- Price * Quantity?","A processing rectangle."),
    unit("REPRESENTATIONS-TO-CODE","Write pseudocode from a flowchart",ids(7,2),[
      "Begin at the start symbol and follow the arrows. Translate each input, process and output into its corresponding statement. At a decision, retain the full expression and place each path's operations in the appropriate block. Declare suitable types for the data used by the resulting program.",
      "In the displayed countdown, a false condition goes directly to the final message. A true condition outputs the current value, decreases it and returns to the same test. That control flow corresponds to WHILE, with the message placed after ENDWHILE.",
    ],[diagramFor("countdown"),example("countdown","Recover a loop from directed paths","Write equivalent pseudocode for the countdown flowchart; Count is a non-negative integer.","Starting at 3 gives 3, 2, 1, Finished. Starting at 0 skips the body and gives Finished.")],
    "The backward arrow must become repetition; writing the body once loses the loop.","Where does the No path from Count > 0 lead?","To OUTPUT \"Finished\", then the end terminator."),
    unit("REPRESENTATIONS-TO-FLOW","Draw a flowchart from a written algorithm",ids(7,3),[
      "When drawing from structured English or pseudocode, identify the sequence, branch boundaries and loop boundaries before choosing symbols. Put a complete condition in every decision and show where execution continues after each path. Convert implicit loop control into visible initialisation, a condition and an update where needed.",
      "A FOR loop that totals four values can be drawn using Index initialised to 1, a decision Index <= 4 and an increment after each addition. The false path outputs the final total. This expands the loop notation while preserving exactly four input operations.",
    ],[diagramFor("sumFour"),worked("Expand a count-controlled loop",[["Source pseudocode",codeFor("sumFour")],["Initial state","Set Total to 0 and Index to 1 before the diamond."],["Repeated path","For a true Index <= 4, input Value, add it, increment Index and return to the test."],["Exit path","After the fourth addition, Index becomes 5; the No path outputs Total and ends."]])],
    "A flowchart answer must contain connected symbols and labelled branches, not only a list of the required shapes.","Does the flowchart request a fifth value when Index is 5?","No. The condition is false, so it exits to the output."),
  ],
  practice:[
    q(6,1,"Write pseudocode for this structured English: read an integer temperature; if it is below zero display Frost, otherwise display Clear; finish after one message.",ids(7,1,2),["Temperature is input as an integer.","The decision tests Temperature < 0.","The true and false branches output \"Frost\" and \"Clear\" respectively."],"Zero belongs to the Clear branch.",{...answer("cold"),conversion:"english-to-pseudocode"}),
    q(6,2,"Write pseudocode equivalent to the supplied flowchart. Level is a whole-number percentage between 0 and 100. State the output for Level 20.",ids(7,2),["The program inputs Level and tests Level < 20.","The two branches output \"Refill\" or \"No action\" as drawn.","Level 20 follows No and outputs No action."],"Copy the strict comparison exactly.",{...stimulusDiagram("refill"),...answer("refill"),conversion:"flowchart-to-pseudocode"}),
    q(6,3,"Draw a flowchart for this structured English: input a person's height in whole centimetres; if the height is at least 120 output Enter, otherwise output Wait; end after that output.",ids(7,3),["Start leads to an input parallelogram for Height.","A decision diamond tests Height >= 120 with labelled branches.","Yes leads to OUTPUT \"Enter\" and No to OUTPUT \"Wait\" in output symbols.","Both outcomes connect forward to the end terminator."],"Height 120 must follow the entry branch.",{...drawing("height"),conversion:"english-to-flowchart"}),
    q(6,4,"Draw a flowchart equivalent to the supplied pseudocode. Show the accumulator initialisation, loop condition, counter update and final output explicitly.",ids(7,3),["Total starts at 0 and Index at 1 before the loop condition.","The decision Index <= 4 admits exactly four iterations.","Each true path inputs Value and adds it to Total.","Index increments and the arrow returns to the decision.","The false path outputs Total and then ends."],"The exit path must lead to the output, not backwards from it.",{...supplied("sumFour"),...drawing("sumFour"),conversion:"pseudocode-to-flowchart"}),
  ],
  authoredExamQuestions:[
    e(6,1,"Write pseudocode for this structured English: input an integer Temperature; display Low if it is below 10, High if it is above 30, and Normal otherwise; output exactly one message. State the message at Temperature 30.",ids(7,2),["Temperature is input before either comparison.","A Temperature < 10 branch outputs \"Low\".","Otherwise, Temperature > 30 outputs \"High\" and the remaining case outputs \"Normal\".","Temperature 30 outputs Normal, with no second message."],"Preserve the three mutually exclusive outcomes, including both boundaries.",{...answer("temperatureBand"),conversion:"english-to-pseudocode"}),
    e(6,2,"Write structured English describing the supplied flowchart, then write equivalent pseudocode. State the classification for an age of 19.",ids(7,1,2),["The description reads an age, classifies ages at least 18 as Adult and all other permitted ages as Minor, then finishes.","The pseudocode inputs Age and uses Age >= 18.","The true path outputs \"Adult\"; the false path outputs \"Minor\".","Age 19 is classified as Adult."],"The condition is not equality to 18.",{...stimulusDiagram("age"),...answer("age"),conversion:"flowchart-to-pseudocode"}),
    e(6,3,"Draw a flowchart for the supplied countdown pseudocode. Indicate the path taken when the initial Count is zero.",ids(7,3),["Start leads to input of Count and a Count > 0 decision.","The Yes path outputs Count and subtracts one before returning to the decision.","The No path outputs \"Finished\" and reaches the end.","An initial zero follows No immediately without outputting a number."],"The decrement must lie on the return path.",{...supplied("countdown"),...drawing("countdown"),conversion:"pseudocode-to-flowchart"}),
  ],
  summary:[["Preserve behaviour","Keep every operator, branch action and input/output order during conversion."],["Read the arrows","Recover the path actually drawn, including loop returns and the final exit."],["Draw the control flow","Use standard symbols, labelled decisions and connected paths; trace boundary cases."]],
};

const refinement = {
  title:"Stepwise refinement to programmable detail",
  subtitle:"Expand a high-level task through successive levels until every leaf operation can be implemented.",
  guidingQuestion:"What decision would a programmer still have to invent at the current level of detail?",
  diagnostic:{prompt:"Is 'work out the average' ready to program if neither the number of values nor the calculation has been specified?",answer:"No. The input count, accumulation and divisor must be defined."},
  units:[
    unit("REFINEMENT-LEVELS","Expand the unresolved operations",ids(8,1),[
      "Stepwise refinement repeatedly expands an algorithm's high-level steps into more detailed steps. Start with the required result, replace an unresolved operation with a sequence that achieves it, and continue until the remaining actions can be programmed without inventing rules.",
      "Decomposition identifies smaller sub-problems; refinement develops the internal detail of a chosen solution. They can be used together. Refining an average calculation means defining how values are read, how a total is updated and when division occurs, rather than merely naming input, process and output.",
    ],[table("Successive levels for three-score averaging",["Level","Description"],[["Goal","Report the arithmetic mean of three scores"],["First expansion","Obtain scores; calculate mean; display mean"],["Further expansion","Set Total to 0; repeat input and addition three times; divide Total by 3; output Average"],["Programmable operations","Use explicit declarations, FOR/NEXT, INPUT, assignment and OUTPUT"]]),flow("Refine one unfinished leaf at a time",[["Inspect","Find a step whose rule or order is still unspecified."],["Expand","Replace it with enough smaller operations to perform that task."],["Reconnect","Keep the surrounding inputs, dependencies and outputs consistent."],["Stop","Check that every leaf has an implementable operation and stated conditions."]])],
    "Adding more headings is not refinement if the same unknown calculations remain hidden.","Which leaf still needs refinement: Total <- Total + Score or process the scores?","Process the scores, because it does not define the required processing."),
    unit("REFINEMENT-ENDPOINT","Reach a complete implementation plan",ids(8,1),[
      "A useful endpoint states the inputs, initial values, arithmetic, decisions, repetition and outputs required by the task. A programmer should be able to translate the resulting pseudocode directly. The refinement must preserve the original requirement at every level.",
      "Check the final version with concrete data. For three-score averaging, reading all three values and dividing once by three distinguishes the intended mean from a running sum, a last-value calculation or an off-by-one loop. Assumptions about permitted input types and ranges should be stated separately from unfinished operations.",
    ],[table("Resolve the remaining questions",["Unresolved point","Decision for this task"],[["How many scores?","Exactly three integers"],["Initial total?","Zero before repetition"],["How to combine?","Add each new score"],["How to average?","Divide completed sum by three"],["When to report?","Once after calculating Average"]]),example("averageThree","The final refined algorithm","Read exactly three integer scores and output their arithmetic mean.","6, 8 and 10 give Total 24 and Average 8; three zeroes give Average 0.")],
    "A leaf called calculate mean remains too vague when the task asks for the level of detail from which it may be programmed.","Why check an all-zero set as well as mixed values?","It checks that initialisation and accumulation do not introduce an unintended non-zero amount."),
  ],
  practice:[
    q(7,1,"Develop successive refinements of 'calculate the total cost of four entered prices' through one intermediate level to programmable operations. State where the total is initialised and output.",ids(8,1),["An intermediate level separates obtaining prices, accumulating their sum and reporting it.","Set Total to 0 before processing any price.","Repeat INPUT Price and Total <- Total + Price exactly four times.","OUTPUT Total occurs after the repetition."],"Give actual operations at the final level, not another vague heading."),
    q(7,2,"A taxi charges 5.00 for up to and including 3 km, plus 2.00 for each kilometre beyond 3. Develop fare calculation into complete pseudocode for a non-negative distance, allowing fractional kilometres.",ids(8,1),["Input Distance using a type that allows fractions.","Test Distance <= 3.","Use Fare <- 5 on the true path and Fare <- 5 + (Distance - 3) * 2 on the other.","Output Fare after the decision."],"Only the distance beyond 3 incurs the extra rate.",answer("taxi")),
    q(7,3,"An intermediate design reads three marks and says 'find and show their mean'. Identify two unresolved operations and replace each with an explicit step.",ids(8,1),["The calculation is unresolved: assign Mean <- (Mark1 + Mark2 + Mark3) / 3.","The reporting operation is unresolved: OUTPUT Mean."],"Retain the stated three inputs and arithmetic mean."),
  ],
  authoredExamQuestions:[
    e(7,1,"Develop successive refinements of 'process a badge order', giving an intermediate IPO outline and then explicit pseudocode. A badge costs 2.20 and every order has a fixed handling charge of 1.50. Assume a positive integer quantity and output only the amount due.",ids(8,1),["The outline identifies quantity input, amount calculation and amount output.","INPUT Quantity obtains the order size.","AmountDue <- Quantity * 2.20 + 1.50 defines the complete rule.","OUTPUT AmountDue presents the required result."],"Charge handling once per order rather than once per badge."),
    e(7,2,"A rainfall algorithm must read five non-negative daily amounts and output their total. Its refinement currently says 'repeat input, add rain, display total'. Explain two missing details and give the corrected final sequence.",ids(8,1),["The total needs an initial value of zero before any addition.","The repetition count and placement of the final output must be explicit.","Set Total <- 0, then repeat INPUT Rain and Total <- Total + Rain exactly five times.","OUTPUT Total follows the completed loop."],"A final five-day total should not be displayed inside every iteration."),
    e(7,3,"Explain why replacing 'calculate delivery charge' with 'calculate the correct delivery charge' is insufficient refinement. For a rule of 4.00 up to and including 2 kg and 6.50 above 2 kg, give the missing decision and branch assignments.",ids(8,1),["The replacement still gives no operations that implement the charge rule.","The condition is Mass <= 2.","The true branch assigns Charge <- 4.00 and the false branch assigns Charge <- 6.50."],"A more emphatic adjective adds no programmable detail."),
  ],
  summary:[["Refine repeatedly","Expand unfinished operations until their rules and order are explicit."],["Preserve the task","Each expansion must still implement the original requirements."],["Reach an endpoint","Complete pseudocode and a trace show whether the final detail is sufficient to program."]],
};

const logic = {
  title:"Logic statements and boundary conditions",
  subtitle:"Translate conditions with relational operators, AND, OR and NOT, then test their truth values.",
  guidingQuestion:"Which exact input combinations should make this part of the algorithm true?",
  diagnostic:{prompt:"For a permitted range from 10 to 20 inclusive, does Value >= 10 OR Value <= 20 exclude anything?",answer:"No. Every number satisfies at least one of those comparisons. Both bounds must hold, so the range uses AND."},
  units:[
    unit("LOGIC-COMPARISONS","Translate relational boundaries",ids(9,1),[
      "A relational expression compares values and produces TRUE or FALSE. Use = for equal, <> for not equal, < and > for strict bounds, and <= or >= when the boundary is included. Read words such as at least, at most, below and above before selecting an operator.",
      "Logical conditions define where an algorithm takes an action or accepts a value. Test the value at the boundary and nearby values on either side. For an integer age requirement of at least 18, 17 must fail while 18 and 19 must pass.",
    ],[table("Words determine the comparison",["Requirement","Expression","Boundary result"],[["At least 18","Age >= 18","18 is TRUE"],["Below zero","Temperature < 0","0 is FALSE"],["At most 100","Mark <= 100","100 is TRUE"],["Not equal to zero","Divisor <> 0","0 is FALSE"]])],
    "Equality at one value is not a substitute for an inclusive lower bound.","What expresses a quantity greater than five?","Quantity > 5; Quantity >= 5 would also admit five."),
    unit("LOGIC-COMBINE","Combine whole conditions",ids(9,1),[
      "AND is true only when both operands are true. OR is true when either or both operands are true. NOT reverses a Boolean value. Combine complete comparisons and use parentheses to make the intended grouping clear.",
      "Eligibility requiring adulthood and no suspension is (Age >= 18) AND NOT Suspended. A discount available to a child or any member is (Age < 12) OR Member. The English requirement determines the connector; choosing OR where both restrictions are required changes who qualifies.",
    ],[table("Truth values of the logical operators",["A","B","A AND B","A OR B","NOT A"],[["FALSE","FALSE","FALSE","FALSE","TRUE"],["FALSE","TRUE","FALSE","TRUE","TRUE"],["TRUE","FALSE","FALSE","TRUE","FALSE"],["TRUE","TRUE","TRUE","TRUE","FALSE"]]),example("eligible","Implement a combined eligibility rule","Input a non-negative integer Age and a Boolean Suspended. Output whether the person is at least 18 and not suspended.","(18, FALSE) gives TRUE; (17, FALSE) and (19, TRUE) give FALSE.")],
    "Write both comparisons in a range: Temperature >= -20 AND <= 50 omits the second operand's identifier.","If both operands are true, is OR false?","No. OR is inclusive here and is true when both are true."),
    unit("LOGIC-CHECK","Test ranges and their complements",ids(9,1),[
      "An inclusive range needs the lower and upper comparisons joined by AND. Values outside that range satisfy a below-lower or above-upper test joined by OR. Negating the entire valid-range expression gives the same invalid-range condition.",
      "Use a table of chosen inputs and expected truth values before putting a condition into a larger algorithm. Include both endpoints, one value outside each endpoint and an internal value. For compound eligibility rules, also vary each Boolean input so a missing NOT or wrong connector cannot hide behind one successful example.",
    ],[table("Accept integer temperatures from -20 to 50",["Temperature","Temperature >= -20","Temperature <= 50","Both hold?"],[[-21,"FALSE","TRUE","FALSE"],[-20,"TRUE","TRUE","TRUE"],[15,"TRUE","TRUE","TRUE"],[50,"TRUE","TRUE","TRUE"],[51,"TRUE","FALSE","FALSE"]]),example("range","Express a permitted interval","Read an integer Temperature and output whether it lies from -20 to 50 inclusive.","The endpoint values -20 and 50 are accepted; -21 and 51 are rejected.")],
    "Do not test only an interior value; incorrect strict operators often agree there and fail at the endpoints.","Write the condition for a temperature outside this permitted interval.","(Temperature < -20) OR (Temperature > 50)."),
  ],
  practice:[
    q(8,1,"Write a logic expression that is true when Mark is between 0 and 100 inclusive. State its values for -1, 0, 100 and 101.",ids(9,1),["(Mark >= 0) AND (Mark <= 100) requires both inclusive bounds.","The four truth values are FALSE, TRUE, TRUE and FALSE."],"OR would admit out-of-range values."),
    q(8,2,"Write a condition for a discount given to anyone under 12 or anyone with Member TRUE. Complete the result column for the supplied cases.",ids(9,1),["(Age < 12) OR Member expresses the rule.","The rows (11, FALSE), (12, FALSE) and (30, TRUE) give TRUE, FALSE and TRUE."],"Membership qualifies regardless of age in this rule.",{table:table("Discount cases",["Age","Member","Discount?"],[[11,"FALSE","?"],[12,"FALSE","?"],[30,"TRUE","?"]]),...answer("discount")}),
    q(8,3,"Write a condition for login when PasswordCorrect is TRUE and Locked is FALSE. Explain why replacing AND with OR changes the rule.",ids(9,1),["PasswordCorrect AND NOT Locked requires the correct password and an unlocked account.","Using OR would allow an unlocked account even when the password is wrong, or a locked account with a correct password."],"NOT must apply to Locked, not PasswordCorrect."),
  ],
  authoredExamQuestions:[
    e(8,1,"A parcel is accepted only if its mass is at least 2 kg and at most 5 kg. Write a logic expression for acceptance and give the truth values for masses 1.9, 2, 5 and 5.1.",ids(9,1),["(Mass >= 2) AND (Mass <= 5) uses the two inclusive limits.","Masses 1.9 and 5.1 produce FALSE.","Masses 2 and 5 produce TRUE."],"Acceptance requires both limits simultaneously."),
    e(8,2,"An alarm sounds when Smoke is TRUE or when Temperature exceeds 60 and Override is FALSE. Write the condition with explicit grouping, then evaluate Smoke FALSE, Temperature 61, Override TRUE.",ids(9,1),["Smoke OR ((Temperature > 60) AND NOT Override) preserves the two alternatives.","With the stated inputs, the temperature branch is false because Override is true.","Smoke is also false, so the overall result is FALSE."],"Override controls the temperature branch only in this requirement."),
    e(8,3,"A design uses (Age >= 16) OR (Age <= 25) to accept ages from 16 to 25 inclusive. Explain the defect using ages 10 and 30, then write a corrected condition and its complement.",ids(9,1),["Age 10 passes the upper comparison and age 30 passes the lower comparison, so both incorrectly qualify under OR.","The correct acceptance condition is (Age >= 16) AND (Age <= 25).","The complement is (Age < 16) OR (Age > 25)."],"Negating a range changes both the comparison directions and the connector."),
  ],
  summary:[["Read the boundary","Use strict or inclusive comparisons according to the stated words."],["Combine deliberately","AND requires every condition; OR allows alternatives; NOT reverses truth."],["Challenge the rule","Test endpoints, outside values and relevant Boolean combinations."]],
};

const integrated = {
  title:"Integrated design: a ticket purchase",
  subtitle:"Connect decomposition, IPO and refinement in one complete, traceable purchase algorithm.",
  guidingQuestion:"Do the module responsibilities, calculations and final pseudocode implement the same purchase rules?",
  diagnostic:{prompt:"Two tickets cost 25.00 each before a 10% student discount. What are the subtotal and final total?",answer:"Subtotal is 50.00; Total is 50.00 × 0.90 = 45.00. Quantity is applied once when calculating the subtotal."},
  units:[
    unit("INTEGRATED-MODULES","Decompose the purchase requirements",ids(2,1,2),[
      "This simplified purchase handles one request for identical tickets. Each ticket costs 25.00. Quantity must be at least one and no greater than PlacesLeft. A Boolean Student choice gives 10% off the whole subtotal. After displaying the total, accept payment only when Paid is at least Total; confirm, give change and reduce PlacesLeft only after sufficient payment.",
      "Assume integer Quantity and PlacesLeft, non-negative PlacesLeft and Paid, and correctly typed inputs. Decompose the work into checking quantity, calculating the charge and completing payment. The charge function can return the amount due; a completion procedure can report the outcome and update the available places. The detailed algorithm below expands these responsibilities inline so every operation is visible.",
    ],[table("Responsibilities and interfaces",["Module responsibility","Receives","Result or effect"],[["CheckQuantity (function)","Quantity, PlacesLeft","Returns whether the request fits availability"],["CalculateCharge (function)","Quantity, Student","Returns amount due using TicketPrice 25.00"],["CompletePayment (procedure)","Quantity, PlacesLeft, Total, Paid","Confirms and updates places on sufficient payment; otherwise reports failure"]]),worked("Trace the agreement between modules",[["Request","Quantity 2, PlacesLeft 8, Student TRUE."],["Quantity decision","The requested 2 tickets fit the available 8."],["Charge result","Two tickets give 50.00 before discount and 45.00 due."],["Payment result","Paid 50.00 gives change 5.00 and leaves 6 places after confirmation."]])],
    "A module boundary must preserve the values another part needs; calculating a charge does not itself confirm payment.","Should insufficient payment reduce PlacesLeft?","No. The stated rule updates availability only after a successful purchase."),
    unit("INTEGRATED-IPO","Keep the quantities and amounts consistent",ids(5,1),[
      "Derive the purchase calculations from the rules before placing them into pseudocode. Subtotal is the ticket count multiplied by the fixed price. Apply the discount to that subtotal once; Total is either 90% of Subtotal or Subtotal itself. Change subtracts the amount due from the amount paid.",
      "The input path depends on earlier decisions. An unavailable request ends with Unavailable, without asking for a student choice or payment. A valid request obtains Student, calculates and displays Total, and then obtains Paid. Keeping those dependencies explicit prevents using payment or discount data on a path that never reads it.",
    ],[table("One consistent worked purchase",["Item","Rule or value","Result"],[["Quantity and price","2 tickets at 25.00","Subtotal = 50.00"],["Student discount","50.00 × 0.90","Total = 45.00"],["Payment","Paid = 50.00; 50.00 - 45.00","Change = 5.00"],["Availability after success","8 - 2","PlacesLeft = 6"]]),flow("Follow the input dependencies",[["Request","Input Quantity and PlacesLeft; check availability."],["Valid request","Input Student; calculate and output Total."],["Payment","Input Paid only after displaying the amount due."],["Outcome","Confirm with change and updated places, or report insufficient payment."]])],
    "Multiplying Subtotal by Quantity again double-counts the number of tickets.","For two non-student tickets, what is Total?","50.00, because Total equals the already calculated Subtotal."),
    unit("INTEGRATED-REFINEMENT","Finish the algorithm and challenge its branches",ids(8,1),[
      "Refine check request into two comparisons joined by AND. Refine calculate charge into multiplication followed by a student decision. Refine complete payment into a sufficient-payment comparison with separate success and failure actions. These expansions resolve each high-level responsibility into programmable statements.",
      "Trace successful and rejected cases against the original rules. Zero quantity and requests above availability must exit before charging. Exact payment is sufficient and gives zero change. Underpayment must preserve the available-place count. The final pseudocode provides a complete endpoint, with no placeholder such as process payment left unresolved.",
    ],[table("Cases that distinguish the branches",["Input case","Expected outcome"],[["Quantity 0, PlacesLeft 8","Unavailable"],["Quantity 9, PlacesLeft 8","Unavailable"],["Quantity 1, PlacesLeft 1, Student FALSE, Paid 25","Total 25; Confirmed; Change 0; PlacesLeft 0"],["Quantity 2, PlacesLeft 8, Student FALSE, Paid 49","Total 50; Insufficient payment; PlacesLeft remains 8"]]),example("ticket","A complete purchase algorithm","Implement the rules and input assumptions in this lesson for one ticket request.","For 2 tickets, 8 places, Student TRUE and Paid 50, the outputs are 45, Confirmed, 5 and 6, in that order.")],
    "A successful price calculation does not imply successful payment; the stock update belongs only on the payment-success path.","Which comparison admits exact payment?","Paid >= Total; using Paid > Total would incorrectly reject exact payment."),
  ],
  practice:[
    q(9,1,"For the purchase in this lesson, describe the data passed into charge calculation and the result passed to payment handling. Explain why printing the charge without returning or otherwise supplying its value is insufficient.",[...ids(2,1,2)], ["Charge calculation needs Quantity and Student, with the fixed TicketPrice available.","The calculated Total must be supplied to payment handling.","The payment comparison needs the numeric amount; display text alone does not provide that interface."],"Distinguish reporting an amount from making it available to another module."),
    q(9,2,"Calculate the results using the lesson's ticket rules for Quantity 3, PlacesLeft 7, Student FALSE and Paid 80. Calculate Subtotal, Total, Change and the new PlacesLeft, and state the output sequence.",ids(5,1),["Subtotal is 3 * 25 = 75 and Total is 75 without a student discount.","Change is 80 - 75 = 5.","PlacesLeft becomes 7 - 3 = 4.","The outputs are 75, Confirmed, 5 and 4."],"Do not multiply the subtotal by three a second time."),
    q(9,3,"Develop the final payment step of the lesson's purchase algorithm into explicit operations. Explain the result for Total 45, Paid 44, Quantity 2 and PlacesLeft 8.",ids(8,1),["Compare Paid >= Total.","On success, calculate Change <- Paid - Total and PlacesLeft <- PlacesLeft - Quantity, then output confirmation and both values.","On failure, output \"Insufficient payment\" without changing PlacesLeft.","The supplied underpayment follows the failure branch and leaves 8 places."],"Place the availability update inside the successful-payment branch."),
  ],
  authoredExamQuestions:[
    e(9,1,"A museum hires audio guides at 4.00 each and requires a refundable deposit of 10.00 per order. It checks available guides, calculates the payment due and records an accepted hire. Describe three corresponding modules, including a suitable interface for the charge calculation.",ids(2,1,2),["An availability module receives requested Quantity and GuidesLeft and determines whether the request can be met.","A charge function receives Quantity and returns Quantity * 4.00 + 10.00.","A recording module uses the accepted quantity and payment outcome to record the hire.","The returned amount is available to the payment check rather than being recalculated independently."],"The deposit is per order, not per guide."),
    e(9,2,"A venue sells tickets at 12.00 each with no discounts. Write an IPO table and pseudocode for a positive quantity and sufficient Paid amount. Output the total and change. State both outputs for Quantity 3 and Paid 50.",ids(5,1),["Inputs are Quantity and Paid; processing multiplies quantity by 12 and subtracts the total from Paid; outputs are Total and Change.","The pseudocode obtains both inputs before using them.","Total <- Quantity * 12 and Change <- Paid - Total implement the rules.","OUTPUT Total, Change gives 36 and 14 for the supplied case."],"This scenario has its own ticket price and no student discount."),
    e(9,3,"Develop complete pseudocode for a pool purchase. The pool sells one session pass at 6.00 when PlacesLeft is positive. Implement these rules: input the non-negative integer PlacesLeft; if no place remains output \"Full\"; otherwise input non-negative Paid, accept at least 6.00, output change and reduce PlacesLeft by one; on underpayment output \"Not paid\" and leave PlacesLeft unchanged. Output the updated PlacesLeft only after success.",ids(8,1),["INPUT PlacesLeft is followed by a decision PlacesLeft > 0.","The no-place branch outputs \"Full\" without reading payment.","The available branch inputs Paid and tests Paid >= 6.00.","Success computes Change <- Paid - 6.00 and PlacesLeft <- PlacesLeft - 1.","Success outputs Change and PlacesLeft; failure outputs \"Not paid\" without the update."],"The rejected payment must not consume a place.",answer("pool")),
  ],
  summary:[["Connect the modules","Give each responsibility the data it needs and make its result available to the next task."],["Trace the same rules","Keep price, discount, payment and availability calculations consistent across every representation."],["Refine every branch","Specify accepted and rejected paths fully, including when state may change."]],
};

const lessons = [abstraction, decomposition, algorithms, ipo, constructs, representations, refinement, logic, integrated];
export function authorSection9Lesson(source) {
  if (source.section === 9) {
    const authored = lessons[source.originalLesson - 46];
    if (!authored) throw new Error(`Missing S9 lesson ${source.originalLesson}`);
    return { ...source, ...authored, summaryMode: "authored",
      teachingCheckpoints:["Use the opening diagnostic before teaching the new material.","Trace the worked example and attempt each unit check before revealing its answer.","Complete practice before attempting the independent exam-style tasks; compare rules, working and results."],
      sources:["Cambridge 9618 2027–2029 syllabus, sections 9.1–9.2: https://www.cambridgeinternational.org/Images/721397-2027-2029-syllabus.pdf","Cambridge 9618 2027–2029 pseudocode guide: https://www.cambridgeinternational.org/Images/721401-2027-2029-pseudocode-guide.pdf","Original teaching scenarios, diagrams, questions and marking points."],
    };
  }
  if (source.kind !== "review" || source.paper !== 2) return source;
  const r = (...ns) => ns.map(n=>`S9.${String(n).padStart(2,"0")}.R`);
  const review = unit("REVIEW","Section 9: Algorithm design and problem-solving",r(1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8,9),[
    "Begin with the required result and retain the facts that determine it. Decompose the task into cooperating responsibilities, choose meaningful identifiers and refine the processing until the rules and execution order are explicit. The resulting IPO, pseudocode and flowchart should describe the same solution.",
    "Challenge a completed algorithm at decision boundaries and loop limits. Follow each path using actual values, checking initialisation, new inputs, updates and the final output. A convincing design combines correct logic with a representation that another programmer can implement without guessing.",
  ],[table("S9 review route",["Design stage","Evidence"],[["Abstraction and decomposition","A purpose-specific model and modules with interfaces"],["Algorithms and identifiers","Defined steps and a name/type/purpose table"],["IPO and constructs","Complete statements with correct branch and loop placement"],["Representations and refinement","Equivalent flowchart/pseudocode and no unresolved processing"],["Logic","Conditions verified at inclusive and exclusive boundaries"]]),example("review","Count accepted parcels","Input four real-valued parcel masses and count those from 2 to 5 kg inclusive.","1.9, 2, 5 and 5.1 produce an accepted count of 2.")],"A correct count for one input set does not establish that both inclusive boundaries are handled correctly.","Why do masses 2 and 5 both qualify?","The stated interval includes both endpoints, so the two comparisons use >= and <=.");
  review.syllabusId = "REVIEW-2-1";
  const reviewQuestions = [
    question("REV-P2-S9-Q1","A parcel desk only decides whether a parcel mass is from 2 to 5 kg inclusive and counts accepted parcels. State which mass data its abstract model needs, give one irrelevant detail for this decision, and decompose the task into three responsibilities.",r(1,2),["The model retains the mass of each parcel in kilograms.","A sender's preferred envelope colour does not affect the stated mass rule.","The task can be divided into obtaining a mass, deciding acceptance and accumulating/reporting the accepted count."],"Retain the mass rule and count rather than inventing a price calculation."),
    question("REV-P2-S9-Q2","Write an identifier table for the supplied parcel-counting algorithm and describe its input, processing and output. Explain why its steps are sufficiently defined to follow.",r(3,4,5),["Index, Weight and Accepted represent the loop position, current parcel mass and qualifying total, with types INTEGER, REAL and INTEGER.","It inputs four masses, applies the stated interval to each, and outputs the number accepted.","The code explicitly defines the iteration count, comparisons and increment, so no acceptance or counting rule remains unspecified."],"Weight must permit fractional kilograms.",supplied("review")),
    question("REV-P2-S9-Q3","Write pseudocode for this structured English: initialise a count to zero; read four parcel masses; for each mass from 2 to 5 kg inclusive increase the count; display the final count. Identify the three basic constructs and state the output for 1.9, 2, 5 and 5.1.",r(6,7,9),["Initialisation precedes a four-iteration loop with a fresh input each time.","The selection uses (Weight >= 2) AND (Weight <= 5) and increments only when true.","The final output follows repetition; initialisation/output illustrate sequence, IF illustrates selection and FOR illustrates iteration.","The supplied masses produce 2."],"The interval requires AND, with both endpoints included.",answer("review")),
    question("REV-P2-S9-Q4","An intermediate parcel design says 'check the mass and update the count'. Refine this step into programmable detail and explain why setting the count to zero belongs outside the four-parcel loop.",r(8),["IF (Weight >= 2) AND (Weight <= 5) THEN Accepted <- Accepted + 1 ENDIF defines the check and update.","Accepted starts at zero before the loop so later qualifying parcels add to earlier ones rather than replacing their count."],"Initialisation inside the loop would lose earlier acceptances."),
  ];
  // The inherited review assigned objectives round-robin across section headings.
  // Repair ownership for every lane so moving S9 IDs does not strand the others.
  const units = source.units.map((u) => {
    const section = Number(u.heading.match(/^Section (\d+):/)?.[1]);
    if (section === 9) return review;
    const objectiveIds = source.objectives.filter(([id])=>id.startsWith(`S${section}.`)).map(([id])=>id);
    return {...u,objectiveIds,materials:u.materials.map(m=>({...m,objectiveIds}))};
  });
  return {...source,units,practice:[...source.practice.filter(p=>p.id!=="V3-Q-L090-03"),...reviewQuestions]};
}
